  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 218 AM MDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nScattered showers continue across portions of Idaho early this \nmorning as an area of low pressure pinwheels eastward across Nevada, \npushing Pacific moisture northward still. This low will continue \nmoving south and east over the next 24 hours and slowly take the \nmoisture with it. So, expect coverage of these showers to also move \nsouth and eastward with the Central Mountains being mainly dry \ntoday. By this evening, most of our rain will come to an end in the \nSnake River Plain, too, with showers lingering in parts of the \nEastern Highlands through Wednesday morning. For most of us, these \nspotty showers today and into tomorrow morning mean about another \ntenth of an inch of QPF at best with some locally higher amounts in \nthe higher elevations in the South Hills and Eastern Highlands. Look \nfor a few inches of high elevation snow, too, with some light snow \nmixing at at times early this morning in some of the lower \nelevations. \n\nTemperatures early today will be cold to start, in the \n20s and 30s for most. As such, we have Frost Advisory/Freeze Warning \nout again for the same areas as yesterday. Expect these Frost \nAdvisory and Freeze Warning headlines again tonight into Wednesday \nmorning and remember our Frost/Freeze products generally come to an \nend on October 15th, but stay tuned for any additional information. \nAfternoon highs today will be in the mid 40s for many, but warm into \nthe low to mid 50s in parts of the Central Mountains where we \nfinally dry out today. As we see this drying and clearing trend, we \nwill have to watch areas (especially the Snake River Plain) \nWednesday morning for some dense fog given all of the moisture we've \nhad around here lately.\n\nHigh pressure returns to Eastern Idaho in the second half of the \nweek, meaning we look to stay dry and warm up after Wednesday. Highs \nreturn to the low 60s for many by Thursday afternoon and we should \nbe in for a beautiful weekend in the mid to upper 60s across the \narea.",
